Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to implement per-key stateful streaming logic, rolling window calculations, and safe downstream signaling with offset semantics. It also probes handling of out-of-order arrivals and ensuring at-least-once delivery by committing only after alert emission succeeds.

Key Concepts
- Per-key state management
- Rolling window calculations (60s)
- Threshold-based anomaly detection
- Idempotent downstream writes and offset commits
- Canary test design for spike vs. normal data

Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode for onMessage
class RuleEngine {
  constructor(windowMs=60000) {
    this.windowMs = windowMs;
    this.state = new Map(); // sensorId -> [{ts,value}, ...], plus sum/count
  }
  onMessage(event){
    const {sensorId, ts, value} = event;
    const bucket = this.state.get(sensorId) || {q:[], sum:0, count:0};
    // prune old
    while (bucket.q.length && bucket.q[0].ts <= ts - this.windowMs) {
      const old = bucket.q.shift(); bucket.sum -= old.value; bucket.count--;
    }
    bucket.q.push({ts,value}); bucket.sum += value; bucket.count++;
    const avg = bucket.count ? bucket.sum / bucket.count : value;
    if (value > 1.2 * avg) { this.emitAlert(sensorId, ts, value, avg); }
    this.state.set(sensorId, bucket);
  }
  rollingAvgFor(sensorId){ const bucket = this.state.get(sensorId); return bucket?.count ? bucket.sum / bucket.count : null; }
  emitAlert(sensorId, ts, value, avg){ // write to threshold_alerts sink
    // sink.write({sensorId, ts, value, avg});
  }
  commitOffsets(){ /* commit after sink write */ }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests per-key stateful processing, TTL-based windowing, and idempotence across replays—core KCNA concepts—while exercising cross-event aggregation and emission semantics.

## Key Concepts
- Per-key in-memory or stateful session store with a 30-second TTL
- Idempotent emits using a deterministic sessionId (userId:start)
- Out-of-order arrivals handled via lastEventTs and session boundaries
- At-least-once delivery guaranteed by committing offsets only after emit

## Code Example
```javascript
function onMessage(ctx, msg) {
  const session = sessionStore().getOrCreate(msg.userId, () => ({
    startTs: msg.timestamp,
    endTs: msg.timestamp,
    count: 0,
    lastEventTs: msg.timestamp
  }));
  session.endTs = msg.timestamp;
  session.lastEventTs = msg.timestamp;
  session.count++;
  sessionStore().setTtl(msg.userId, 30000, () => {
    ctx.emit('sessions', {
      userId: msg.userId,
      sessionStart: session.startTs,
      sessionEnd: session.endTs,
      eventCount: session.count
    });
    sessionStore().remove(msg.userId);
  });
}

function commitOffsets() {
  ctx.commit();
}

function sessionStore() {
  return ctx.state();
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to handle schema evolution in a streaming pipeline, ensuring backward/forward compatibility and correct event-time handling under retries.

## Key Concepts
- KCNA versioned deserialization
- Event-time preservation
- Per-id deduplication with TTL
- End-to-end at-least-once semantics
- Canary tests with mixed versions

## Code Example
```javascript
function normalizeAuditEvent(raw){
  const obj = JSON.parse(raw);
  const v = obj.version ?? (obj.actor===undefined ? 1 : 2);
  if(v === 1){
    return { id: obj.id, ts: obj.ts, action: obj.action, actor: null };
  }
  return { id: obj.id, ts: obj.ts, action: obj.action, actor: obj.actor };
}
```

